config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4
	bool "PX4 zenoh-pico CDR egress (rmw_zenoh /fmu/in/... keys)"
	default n
	depends on ZROS
	select PX4_MSGS
	select ZENOH_PICO
	select ZENOH_PICO_PUBLICATION
	select ZENOH_PICO_MULTI_THREAD
	select ZENOH_PICO_LINK_TCP
	help
	  CDR-encoded px4_msgs egress over zenoh-pico to a PX4 (RT1176) target
	  running the zenoh module. Each ZROS sample is mapped to the matching
	  px4_msgs C struct (FLU->FRD, ns->us), serialized with Micro-CDR
	  (00 01 00 00 encapsulation header + IDL-order body), and published on
	  <ns>/fmu/in/<topic>[/RIHS01_<hash>].

	  This is the CDR/rmw_zenoh replacement for the csyn synapse wire; leave
	  SPINALI_SYNAPSE_CSYN and SPINALI_SYNAPSE_ZENOH off when this is on.

if SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4

choice
	prompt "PX4 zenoh session mode"
	default SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_MODE_CLIENT
	help
	  Session topology, selected by build. The CDR wire payload is
	  byte-identical in both; only the transport role and graph behaviour
	  differ.

	  CLIENT (client-server) is required when the peer is a zenoh router
	  (rmw_zenohd) and this node must appear in the ROS2 graph (`ros2 topic
	  list` / `node list`). The node is a CLIENT that CONNECTs to the router
	  at LOCATOR. rmw_zenoh builds the graph from zenoh liveliness tokens
	  under @ros2_lv/**, and every ros2 tool is a LATE JOINER because this
	  node is always-on and boots first. As a CLIENT the router owns this
	  session's tokens and replays them to late joiners on their liveliness
	  queries and history subscriptions. A bare PEER cannot serve that path:
	  zenoh-pico returns early from _z_interest_process_interest() on a
	  unicast link ("Nothing to do on unicast"), so it never re-declares its
	  tokens in response to a forwarded CURRENT|TOKENS Interest, and late
	  joiners never learn its topics.

	  PEER (peer-to-peer) is a direct, router-free unicast link to a plain
	  zenoh-pico/PX4 endpoint where only the CDR payload matters. In this
	  mode THIS NODE IS THE LISTENER: it opens a unicast peer transport and
	  LISTENs on LOCATOR (a local bind address, e.g. tcp/0.0.0.0:7447), and
	  PX4 is the CONNECTOR that dials in. PX4's zenoh glue only ever inserts
	  MODE+CONNECT and cannot LISTEN, so the node must own the listen role;
	  zenoh-pico provides it via Z_FEATURE_UNICAST_PEER + the LISTEN config
	  key (both on by default in the pinned zenoh-pico). No ROS2 graph
	  discovery.

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_MODE_PEER
	bool "Peer-to-peer (node LISTENs, PX4 connects; no router)"

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_MODE_CLIENT
	bool "Client-server (node connects to a zenoh router, e.g. rmw_zenohd)"

endchoice


config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_LOCATOR
	string "Zenoh endpoint locator"
	default "tcp/10.58.0.1:7447"
	help
	  In MODE_CLIENT this is the REMOTE endpoint this node CONNECTs to: the
	  zenoh router (rmw_zenohd) listen address, e.g. tcp/<router_ip>:7447.

	  In MODE_PEER this is the LOCAL bind address this node LISTENs on for
	  the incoming PX4 connection, e.g. tcp/0.0.0.0:7447 (all interfaces) or
	  tcp/<node_ip>:7447 (a specific interface). PX4 is then configured to
	  CONNECT to tcp/<node_ip>:7447.

	  Match the transport (TCP) the far side uses and keep batch/mtu params
	  in agreement.


config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_RUNTIME_CONFIG
	bool "Runtime-configurable net mode + locator (shell, RAM only)"
	default n
	help
	  When set, the session topology (peer/listen vs client/connect) and the
	  locator are no longer fixed by the MODE_PEER/MODE_CLIENT choice and the
	  LOCATOR string at build time; instead they become runtime parameters,
	  mirroring PX4's `zenoh config net peer|client <ip>:<port>`. New shell
	  commands are added under `zenoh_px4`:

	    zenoh_px4 config net peer <ip>:<port>    (node LISTENs on this LOCAL addr)
	    zenoh_px4 config net client <ip>:<port>  (CONNECT to a zenoh router)
	    zenoh_px4 config show                    (print staged/active config)
	    zenoh_px4 restart                        (reopen the session to apply)

	  The MODE_* choice and LOCATOR value above are used as the initial
	  power-on defaults. Changes are staged in RAM and applied on the next
	  session open (explicit `restart`, not live).

	  The runtime config is NOT persisted: a `config net` override is lost on
	  reboot and the build defaults are restored. (An earlier revision saved
	  it to NVS, but writing to the internal flash the CPU executes from
	  stalled the shell thread on the XIP MCXN target during the flash
	  program/erase, so persistence was removed.)

	  NOTE the peer-mode semantic caveat: unlike PX4, where `net peer
	  <ip>:<port>` is the REMOTE connect target, here peer mode makes the node
	  the LISTENER, so the <ip>:<port> is this node's LOCAL bind/listen address
	  (e.g. 0.0.0.0:7447). Client mode matches PX4: it is the remote router.

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_DOMAIN_ID
	int "Zenoh/ROS2 domain id (must match PX4's ZENOH_DOMAIN_ID param)"
	default 0
	range 0 232
	help
	  PX4's zenoh module builds its key expression as
	  <ZENOH_DOMAIN_ID><topic>/<type>/<hash-or-marker> (see
	  generate_rmw_zenoh_topic_keyexpr() in PX4-Autopilot
	  src/modules/zenoh/zenoh.cpp) -- the domain id is concatenated
	  directly in front of the topic path with no separator of its own
	  (the topic string supplies the leading '/'). This must equal the
	  PX4-side ZENOH_DOMAIN_ID parameter (default 0) or every key will
	  mismatch.

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_NAMESPACE
	string "ROS2 namespace segment prepended to fmu/in/<topic>"
	default ""
	help
	  Optional deployment namespace, mirroring rmw_zenoh's ROS_NAMESPACE.
	  Empty publishes <domain_id>/fmu/in/<topic>/... Do not use leading or
	  trailing slashes.

	  NOTE: as of the PX4-Autopilot zenoh module reviewed for this driver,
	  PX4's own key builder (generate_rmw_zenoh_topic_keyexpr()) has no
	  namespace parameter -- its dds_topics.yaml topic strings are always
	  the bare /fmu/in/<topic> form. Setting this to anything non-empty
	  will therefore NOT match a stock PX4 zenoh module; it only matters
	  when the peer is a generic rmw_zenoh/ROS2 node (or a PX4 fork that
	  has been patched to prefix its topics). Leave empty for PX4.

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_RIHS_HASH
	bool "Append RIHS01 type hash to the key expression"
	default y
	help
	  Selects which of PX4's two key expression forms this bridge emits
	  (see generate_rmw_zenoh_topic_keyexpr() in PX4-Autopilot
	  src/modules/zenoh/zenoh.cpp):

	    y: <domain_id><ns>/fmu/in/<topic>/px4_msgs::msg::dds_::<Type>_/RIHS01_<hex>
	    n: <domain_id><ns>/fmu/in/<topic>/px4_msgs::msg::dds_::<Type>_/TypeHashNotSupported

	  This must match PX4's CONFIG_ZENOH_KEY_TYPE_HASH build option
	  exactly (y is the PX4 default, ROS2 Jazzy+); n only for a PX4 build
	  configured for Humble-and-earlier-style keys.

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_RMW_COMPAT
	bool "Emit rmw_zenoh liveliness tokens + per-sample attachments"
	default n
	help
	  When set, the bridge additionally declares an rmw_zenoh liveliness
	  token per topic (so the topic appears in `ros2 topic list`) and puts
	  an rmw_zenoh-format attachment (sequence number, source timestamp,
	  source GID) on every sample (so `ros2 topic echo` delivers it). This
	  targets a generic ROS2 (rmw_zenoh) consumer, NOT stock PX4.

	  Leave n for the PX4/RT1176 target: PX4 reads only the CDR payload and
	  ignores both the attachment slot and liveliness tokens, so the wire
	  payload is byte-identical either way; the extra bytes only cost
	  bandwidth. The liveliness token keyexpr mangling and the attachment
	  encoding are internal rmw_zenoh_cpp conventions, versioned per ROS
	  distro (Jazzy assumed here). They MUST be matched against a reference
	  Wireshark capture from a real rclcpp publisher; a mismatch fails
	  silently (topic missing from `ros2 topic list`, or `ros2 topic echo`
	  stays empty). See drivers/synapse/zenoh_px4/RMW_ZENOH_COMPAT.md.

if SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_RMW_COMPAT

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_RMW_NODE_NAME
	string "ROS2 node name advertised in liveliness tokens"
	default "spinali_t1"

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_RMW_NODE_NS
	string "ROS2 node namespace advertised in liveliness tokens"
	default "/"
	help
	  ROS2 namespace for the advertised node, e.g. "/" for the root
	  namespace. Slashes are mangled to '%' in the token, matching
	  rmw_zenoh.

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_RMW_ENCLAVE
	string "ROS2 security enclave advertised in liveliness tokens"
	default "%"
	help
	  Mangled enclave segment. Default "%" is rmw_zenoh's encoding of the
	  root enclave "/".

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_RMW_QOS
	string "rmw_zenoh QoS keyexpr segment for the published topics"
	default "::,7:,:,:,,"
	help
	  The QoS segment rmw_zenoh appends to the publisher liveliness token.
	  This format is version-specific and the single most fragile field;
	  the entity is DROPPED from the ROS graph (topic absent from
	  `ros2 topic list`) if it does not parse, even though the zenoh
	  liveliness token itself is accepted by the router.

	  The default below was copied VERBATIM from a ROS2 Jazzy `talker`
	  (demo_nodes_cpp) chatter publisher token captured with
	  scripts/rmw_zenoh_liveliness_dump.py; it encodes the rclcpp default
	  publisher QoS (RELIABLE / VOLATILE / KEEP_LAST depth 7). The six
	  comma-separated groups are, in order:
	    <reliability>:<durability>,<history>:<depth>,<deadline>,<lifespan>,<liveliness+lease>
	  with empty subfields meaning "system default / unspecified".

	  Capture your own consumer's expected profile the same way and paste
	  it here if you need best-effort or a different history/depth (a
	  reliability mismatch will list the topic but prevent `ros2 topic
	  echo` from subscribing). See RMW_ZENOH_COMPAT.md.

endif # SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_RMW_COMPAT

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_OPTICAL_FLOW_RATE_HZ
	int "Optical flow publish rate cap (Hz, 0 = off, -1 = unlimited)"
	default -1

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_FLOW_URAD_PER_COUNT
	int "Optical-flow angular resolution (micro-radians per raw count)"
	default 2000
	help
	  Per-count angular resolution of the PAA3905 flow output, in
	  MICRO-radians. The bridge converts the raw integer delta counts into
	  the integrated angular displacement (radians) PX4 expects:

	    pixel_flow[rad] = raw_delta_counts * (this_value * 1e-6)

	  The default (2000 urad/count = 0.002 rad/count) is only a starting
	  point. CALIBRATE it for your optics: command a known translation at a
	  known height, then adjust until EKF2's reported velocity matches ground
	  truth. A wrong value scales optical-flow velocity linearly.

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_DISTANCE_SENSOR_RATE_HZ
	int "Distance sensor publish rate cap (Hz, 0 = off, -1 = unlimited)"
	default -1

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_GNSS_RATE_HZ
	int "Sensor GPS publish rate cap (Hz, 0 = off, -1 = unlimited)"
	default 25

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_MAG_RATE_HZ
	int "Sensor mag publish rate cap (Hz, 0 = off, -1 = unlimited)"
	default 50

config SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4_SENSOR_COMBINED_RATE_HZ
	int "Sensor combined (IMU) publish rate cap (Hz, 0 = off, -1 = unlimited)"
	default 0
	help
	  Publishes the ICM IMU (ZROS topic_imu0) as px4_msgs SensorCombined
	  (FRD, accel+gyro) on <domain>/fmu/in/sensor_combined/... . 0 disables
	  the row (default). For RViz "movement" ~100 Hz is plenty; the on-node
	  IMU runs far faster. gyro/accel integration periods are derived from
	  the message-stamp delta.

	  NOTE on EKF2: this is the topic single-instance EKF2 (SENS_IMU_MODE=1)
	  reads. On a flight controller that already publishes sensor_combined
	  from its own IMU driver, an injected copy competes with the local
	  publisher and will NOT cleanly drive EKF2 (works only on a PX4 with no
	  local IMU / replay-style). It is always fine for visualization. Multi-
	  EKF (SENS_IMU_MODE=0) uses vehicle_imu instead and ignores this topic.

module = SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4
module-str = synapse_zenoh_px4
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # SYNAPSE_ZENOH_PX4
